# C#/.NET patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
C#/.NET design (Framework Design Guidelines, Microsoft async/DI guidance):
async/await, dependency injection, LINQ, and disposal. Applies only to
`*.cs` files.

## Async/await

- Never `async void` except for an actual event handler; every other async
  method returns `Task` or `Task<T>`.
- `Task`/`Task<T>` by default; `ValueTask`/`ValueTask<T>` only for a hot-path
  method that frequently completes synchronously (a cache hit) where the
  allocation is measured to matter; a `ValueTask` is awaited exactly once and
  never stored.
- `.ConfigureAwait(false)` on awaited tasks in library code that does not
  need a captured context. Application-level request-handling code in
  ASP.NET Core does not need it; match what the project's code does.
- Never `.Result`, `.Wait()`, or `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` on a `Task`
  from synchronous code; make the caller `async`. If it truly cannot be
  (a legacy synchronous interface boundary), flag it as a design problem.

## Dependency injection

- Register at the narrowest correct lifetime: `Singleton` for stateless or
  thread-safe shared state, `Scoped` for per-request/unit-of-work state
  (`DbContext`), `Transient` for cheap or intentionally non-shared
  instances. Never capture a `Scoped` service in a `Singleton`; resolve it
  per use via `IServiceScopeFactory`/`IServiceProvider.CreateScope()`.
- Depend on an interface (`IOrderRepository`) only at an I/O or substitution
  boundary that DI resolves (repository, external client, clock). A single
  internal implementation gets no interface.
- Constructor injection for required dependencies; property injection only
  for optional dependencies with a working default.

## LINQ efficiency

- Materialize with `.ToList()`/`.ToArray()` before enumerating a deferred
  query more than once.
- One combined pass instead of stacked `.Where()`/`.Select()` passes over the
  same large in-memory sequence.
- On an EF Core `IQueryable<T>`, keep `.Where` and `.Select` in the query so
  they translate to SQL; no early `.ToList()` then in-memory filtering.
- `.Any()` for existence, never `.Count() > 0`.

## Disposal patterns

- Any `IDisposable` local or field is in a `using` statement/declaration
  (`using var conn = new SqlConnection(...);`) or disposed in `Dispose()`/
  `finally`, never left for the finalizer.
- `await using` for an `IAsyncDisposable`.
- A class owning `IDisposable`/`IAsyncDisposable` fields implements
  `IDisposable`/`IAsyncDisposable` and disposes them itself.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A "god service"/"utils" static class of unrelated helpers.
- Catching `Exception` broadly and continuing, instead of the specific
  exception type or letting it propagate; see `rules/security.mdc` and
  `dotnet-code-review`'s focus list for the review-time version.
- A public mutable `List<T>`/collection property instead of
  `IReadOnlyList<T>`/`IReadOnlyCollection<T>` or a defensive copy.
